1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:08,000
A revolutionary design concept, built to break records by a legion of brave men.

2
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Protected by an aluminum skin, it boasts the highest rooftop in the United States,

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
with its roots deep in the bedrock of Chicago's land and history.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, the Sears Tower on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,140
More than 25,000 people walk into the Sears Tower every day.

6
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:50,120
It takes 104 elevators, some double-decker, to transport workers and visitors to the 110 floors of the tallest building in North America.

7
00:00:52,140 --> 00:00:59,860
The Sears Tower was considered the tallest building in the world until 1998, when the Petronas Towers in Malaysia surpassed it.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,000
by 33 feet, but only with the help of decorative spires.

9
00:01:07,001 --> 00:01:13,180
It rises 1,450 feet above the city of Chicago and has one of the most impressive observation

10
00:01:13,181 --> 00:01:14,680
decks in the world.

11
00:01:14,681 --> 00:01:19,600
When people come up to the Sears Tower Skydeck, on a clear day they're able to see up to 50

12
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:25,480
miles, so you're really not just seeing downtown Chicago, you're also seeing the rest of Illinois,

13
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:30,060
Indiana, Wisconsin, and Michigan, and it's just an amazing, amazing view.

14
00:01:30,380 --> 00:01:32,980
And with the Sears Tower being the tallest building in Chicago,

15
00:01:33,260 --> 00:01:37,980
you're looking down on this amazing architectural mecca that is Chicago.

16
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:45,400
More than a million tourists come to visit the Sky Deck annually

17
00:01:45,401 --> 00:01:48,220
to take a multimedia elevator ride up to the top.

18
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,560
The Sears Tower Observatory has some of the most unique elevators in the world.

19
00:01:53,561 --> 00:01:55,705
First of all, they're two of the fastest.

20
00:01:55,706 --> 00:01:58,320
At top speed, they're going 1,600 feet per minute.

21
00:01:58,820 --> 00:02:03,460
And you start two levels in the basement and come up to the 103rd floor in about 60 seconds.

22
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:11,440
The 4.5 million square foot skyscraper, located in the heart of Chicago's Loop,

23
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:16,645
includes offices, a private dining club, conference facilities, a U.S.

24
00:02:16,646 --> 00:02:19,540
post office, retail stores, and restaurants.

25
00:02:19,541 --> 00:02:25,160
It is a length of close to five football fields.

26
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:29,620
There's 2,232 steps to get to the top of the building.

27
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,260
There's 796 lavatories.

28
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:40,540
It takes 110 janitors, 100 security guards, 25 engineers, three electricians,

29
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:43,860
and dozens more to maintain and operate.

30
00:02:43,861 --> 00:02:56,360
The 50,000 miles of electric wiring, 25,000 miles of plumbing, 80 miles of elevator cable, and 145,000 light fixtures in the Sears Tower.

31
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:10,130
Tony Tonian has worked at the Sears Tower for 13 years and is responsible for operating the six automated window washing rigs that scale the black exterior to wash the

32
00:03:10,131 --> 00:03:11,180
16,100 windows.

33
00:03:11,181 --> 00:03:18,660
For washing the windows, the window head will jut against the window with this first squeegee knocking off the bulk of the heavier dirt.

34
00:03:19,100 --> 00:03:22,420
Then we have spray nozzles behind it that spray water onto the window.

35
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:24,760
Then the brush will knock the rest of the dirt loose.

36
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:29,520
And then we have three squeegees behind that's connected to a vacuum that vacuum the water off the window.

37
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:38,160
As long as the air temperature is above 25 degrees and the winds don't kick up above 30 miles an hour, the windows are washed year-round.

38
00:03:38,161 --> 00:03:52,161
When you think about maintaining the comfort level in a building this size with so many floors, you go on a main street downstairs, it could be 65 degrees.

39
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:59,260
You come up to the 78th floor where I work, it may be only 50 degrees.

40
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,400
There's a difference in temperature.

41
00:04:01,900 --> 00:04:08,400
People don't think that the engineers have to be involved to compensate for this different temperature.

42
00:04:09,060 --> 00:04:13,040
In fact, there is a staff of operating engineers who closely monitor the heating,

43
00:04:13,420 --> 00:04:17,580
ventilating, and air conditioning units located on floors 29 to 33,

44
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:22,940
64 to 66, 88 to 90, and 104 to 109.

45
00:04:22,941 --> 00:04:29,180
These floors are considered hidden because they can only be accessed by the freight elevators.

46
00:04:33,520 --> 00:04:38,220
All the refrigeration for the entire building comes from this location.

47
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:41,500
I have a total of five chillers here.

48
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:46,700
These chillers are equal to 4,800 tons of refrigeration.

49
00:04:46,880 --> 00:04:51,840
Now picture in your apartment or in your bedroom, you have a one-ton air conditioner sitting through your window.

50
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:55,720
One of these chillers are equal to 4,800 of those.

51
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,680
With 3,000 thermostats throughout the building,

52
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:01,940
plus the computers and lights used by the 10,000 tenants,

53
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:06,180
it's a wonder that the Sears Tower has never lost power.

54
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:11,320
In fact, the two standby 2,100 kilowatt generators,

55
00:05:11,840 --> 00:05:14,960
which can supply enough electricity to power 200 homes each,

56
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:16,520
have never been used.

57
00:05:17,340 --> 00:05:21,760
With that much potential power, the Sears Tower was built to last.

58
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:29,080
and in 1968 when Sears Roebuck and Company decided to build a new headquarters for its

59
00:05:29,081 --> 00:05:35,720
growing company it was going to be built big Sears was the dominant retailer in the country

60
00:05:35,721 --> 00:05:40,760
he had been number one for a long time and we far exceeded the competition which in those days was

61
00:05:40,761 --> 00:05:41,461
J.C.

62
00:05:41,462 --> 00:05:48,180
Penney and Montgomery Ward and Sears growth was just phenomenal but it was because after the

63
00:05:48,181 --> 00:05:55,280
Second World War, Sears envisioned the use of the automobile and the advent of the suburbs.

64
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:58,940
So we were building stores like crazy on the fringe of cities.

65
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,340
And if you're going to be Sears, you're going to be the biggest, you certainly can't build

66
00:06:03,341 --> 00:06:05,280
a small building as your headquarters, you know.

67
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:09,060
I mean, if you're the biggest, biggest retailer, the biggest baddest on the block, you need

68
00:06:09,061 --> 00:06:09,761
a big place.

69
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:13,960
They needed the space because they're just a huge company, but I think they were looking

70
00:06:13,961 --> 00:06:15,820
at wanting to make a statement.

71
00:06:15,821 --> 00:06:23,420
About 70 years earlier, this big company got its start in a small town called North Redwood,

72
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:29,140
Minnesota, with a railroad station agent named Richard W. Sears. Bored with his job,

73
00:06:29,800 --> 00:06:33,860
he created a mail order business and then a watch company on the side.

74
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:41,560
In 1887, after moving his business to Chicago, he placed an ad for a skilled watchmaker

75
00:06:41,561 --> 00:06:44,380
and found Alva C. Roebuck.

76
00:06:45,200 --> 00:06:47,100
Later, a partnership formed.

77
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:51,800
And by 1893, the two created Sears Roebuck & Company,

78
00:06:52,940 --> 00:06:56,780
a catalog business that catered mostly to farmers in need of general goods.

79
00:06:57,880 --> 00:06:59,800
So the catalog was right from the beginning.

80
00:07:00,140 --> 00:07:01,660
Everything was done through the mail.

81
00:07:02,340 --> 00:07:05,860
And Richard Sears knew how to do that because of his experience with the railroad.

82
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:09,020
So he knew how to move material and use the mail and that sort of thing.

83
00:07:09,021 --> 00:07:15,020
Business grew so fast that by the turn of the century, Sears leased additional buildings all around Chicago

84
00:07:15,021 --> 00:07:21,280
and constructed a building affectionately named by Sears employees, The Great Works,

85
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:27,860
a 40-acre, $5 million campus-like complex on the residential west side of Chicago.

86
00:07:28,840 --> 00:07:33,480
When the building was built, it actually was the largest commercial space in the United States.

87
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:36,140
And from what I'm saying, it might have been the largest in the world,

88
00:07:36,141 --> 00:07:41,220
which just gives you an idea of just how the magnitude of what Sears was doing,

89
00:07:41,400 --> 00:07:45,600
you know, how their business had expanded by the size of the buildings that they occupied.

90
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:49,800
But even the great works wasn't big enough to contain Sears.

91
00:07:51,440 --> 00:07:56,800
In 1968, with over 7,000 employees scattered around 10 locations in Chicago,

92
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:02,700
Sears chairman Gordon Metcalf decided to bring the company under one very large roof.

93
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:12,580
Since the building was going to be constructed by its owner for the company's own use, Sears planned from the inside out.

94
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:19,920
Before even selecting an architect, they hired SLS Environetics, an interior design firm from New York,

95
00:08:20,540 --> 00:08:23,460
to perform studies on how much space Sears really needed.

96
00:08:24,080 --> 00:08:29,080
We wanted to know how much space would we need, both initially, when we moved in,

97
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:35,920
and also 30 years down the road, because it was planned that this building would be big enough to encompass our growth for 30 years.

98
00:08:35,921 --> 00:08:39,380
and Environetics was very much involved in that process of planning.

99
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:44,720
Then later on, they were involved in determining how the Sears floors would lay out,

100
00:08:45,040 --> 00:08:48,260
how they'd be configured, the relationship between departments and so on.

101
00:08:48,900 --> 00:08:53,160
After conducting interviews with department heads based on detailed questionnaires

102
00:08:53,161 --> 00:08:57,480
and taking meticulous inventory of all the existing furniture and equipment,

103
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:03,660
Environetics fed its data into a computer to analyze the company's total anatomy.

104
00:09:04,360 --> 00:09:07,440
Literally, there were 90 or 200 interviews

105
00:09:07,441 --> 00:09:12,120
that were then converted into space requirements

106
00:09:12,121 --> 00:09:15,500
for every individual listed in those questionnaires.

107
00:09:15,960 --> 00:09:19,380
For example, a buyer would be 150 square feet.

108
00:09:20,120 --> 00:09:22,540
A vice president would be 300 square feet,

109
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,860
whereas a clerical worker would be 50 square feet.

110
00:09:25,861 --> 00:09:27,780
So you had all of these projections,

111
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:30,240
and you moved them out into the future,

112
00:09:30,480 --> 00:09:32,280
and that's what led to Sears deciding

113
00:09:32,281 --> 00:09:37,720
that in 30 years they would need 3.5 million square feet.

114
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:40,360
And that became the size of the project,

115
00:09:40,600 --> 00:09:43,080
even though at the time we were going to move in,

116
00:09:43,180 --> 00:09:44,920
we'd only need 2 million square feet.

117
00:09:45,560 --> 00:09:47,960
Armed with computer-analyzed growth projections,

118
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:49,520
Sears left the suburbs

119
00:09:49,521 --> 00:09:52,140
and chose a 3-acre site in the Garment District

120
00:09:52,141 --> 00:09:53,620
on the southwest edge of the loop

121
00:09:53,621 --> 00:09:55,720
on which to build their future headquarters.

122
00:09:56,360 --> 00:09:58,400
We came downtown, looked at a number of sites,

123
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:01,500
but the one that was finally chosen was almost ideal

124
00:10:01,501 --> 00:10:03,120
in terms of public transportation.

125
00:10:03,400 --> 00:10:07,500
It had access to all the suburban railroads and also the bus network.

126
00:10:07,820 --> 00:10:10,300
So you couldn't probably have found a better place

127
00:10:10,301 --> 00:10:14,940
in terms of letting your employees get to work in the most advantageous way.

128
00:10:16,100 --> 00:10:20,120
They were loyal to Chicago, and so they wanted to stay in Chicago,

129
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:22,400
and they felt that if they stayed in Chicago,

130
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:25,340
they were going to be able to attract the best talent.

131
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:30,300
Sears was looking for talented architects as well as talented employees.

132
00:10:30,301 --> 00:10:34,220
And where better to look than the birthplace of skyscrapers?

133
00:10:35,260 --> 00:10:35,960
Chicago.

134
00:10:37,200 --> 00:10:41,060
After all, where else could one find a designer bold enough

135
00:10:41,061 --> 00:10:43,060
to plan a building great enough

136
00:10:43,061 --> 00:10:46,860
to become the home of the world's biggest store?

137
00:10:49,720 --> 00:10:52,400
The Sears Tower building site had one problem.

138
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:54,020
Quincy Street ran through it.

139
00:10:54,400 --> 00:10:58,560
For $2.7 million, Chicago sold that section of Quincy Street to Sears.

140
00:10:58,561 --> 00:11:00,180
And the problem was solved.

141
00:11:00,300 --> 00:11:03,340
The Sears Tower will return on Modern Marvels.

142
00:11:05,480 --> 00:11:10,220
By the late 60s, Sears had grown to become the number one retailer in the world.

143
00:11:13,520 --> 00:11:18,180
As a result, they had outgrown their current headquarters and were now ready for a new home.

144
00:11:22,600 --> 00:11:25,340
A great deal of work went into choosing the building site.

145
00:11:26,140 --> 00:11:30,000
But the choice of who would design their new home came effortlessly.

146
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:37,860
Skidmore was chosen as the architect because at the time they were the preeminent corporate builders of office buildings in this country.

147
00:11:37,861 --> 00:11:41,860
They had done a number of great buildings and they had the most talent.

148
00:11:42,280 --> 00:11:45,880
That distinction was to the credit of two partners at Skidmore, Owings & Merrill.

149
00:11:47,260 --> 00:11:54,320
Bruce Graham, an innovative and outspoken architect, and Fasler Kahn, a modest but brilliant structural engineer.

150
00:11:55,320 --> 00:12:02,060
Bruce and Foss collaborated quite closely in the design of a number of buildings throughout the 1960s.

151
00:12:02,400 --> 00:12:08,340
And eventually it led to the design of the John Hancock, which is a very tall building.

152
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:13,120
It was this kind of dynamic energy that existed between the two of them.

153
00:12:13,620 --> 00:12:16,600
The two often met outside of work to discuss different ideas.

154
00:12:17,140 --> 00:12:22,060
And while eating lunch one afternoon, they hit on the design that would become the Sears Tower.

155
00:12:22,880 --> 00:12:25,980
We were pursuing the idea of the bundle tube system,

156
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:28,920
and Foss was trying to describe this to Bruce.

157
00:12:29,500 --> 00:12:32,660
And finally Bruce says, I think I understand what you're saying.

158
00:12:33,180 --> 00:12:36,500
And Bruce was a smoker and took out his pack of cigarettes

159
00:12:36,501 --> 00:12:39,880
and took out a number of cigarettes and kind of held them in his hand

160
00:12:39,881 --> 00:12:41,860
and had them coming to different heights

161
00:12:41,861 --> 00:12:44,660
and asked Foss if this, in fact, was his idea,

162
00:12:44,760 --> 00:12:47,480
if this is what he was thinking the system might look like

163
00:12:47,481 --> 00:12:49,880
from an architectural standpoint, from a visual standpoint.

164
00:12:49,881 --> 00:12:51,900
And Foss said, that's exactly right.

165
00:12:52,060 --> 00:12:58,060
The idea of using tube construction was just starting to gain momentum in the architectural world.

166
00:12:59,740 --> 00:13:09,000
Up until about 1960, most buildings were built with very wide spacing of columns and long spans between them.

167
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:10,740
And these were called frame buildings.

168
00:13:11,060 --> 00:13:13,920
And that was the system that was used for many, many years.

169
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:20,960
Steel frame construction relied heavily on internal support columns and bracing to bear all of the structural loads.

170
00:13:22,060 --> 00:13:26,000
The size and number of these support columns directly correlated with the height of the building.

171
00:13:26,620 --> 00:13:33,700
As buildings became taller, we realized that, in fact, this frame system was becoming very inefficient,

172
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:39,560
as can be demonstrated by the Empire State Building with its very large quantity of structural steel.

173
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:46,640
Osler Khan was best known in the architectural world as the father of tubular construction.

174
00:13:46,641 --> 00:13:51,100
His idea was to bring the exterior columns closer together

175
00:13:51,101 --> 00:13:54,660
so that the columns would act like a wall in the perimeter of the building.

176
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:59,620
This would minimize the need for massive support columns in the center of the building.

177
00:14:00,700 --> 00:14:03,900
The Hancock Building, as well as the World Trade Center buildings,

178
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:06,360
were both designed using this new construction method.

179
00:14:09,160 --> 00:14:13,720
The idea for Sears Tower was that since this was such a large building,

180
00:14:13,721 --> 00:14:20,080
the tube started to become inefficient because it was so wide and so deep.

181
00:14:20,360 --> 00:14:27,000
So the idea was, why not take multiple tubes and put them together, bundle them?

182
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:30,120
And so Sears Tower, if you look at a floor plan,

183
00:14:30,600 --> 00:14:36,120
is a series of nine tubes, three by three, that are bundled together.

184
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:39,020
At the base of the building are nine tubes.

185
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:42,620
Two of the tubes end at the 50th floor.

186
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:45,940
Two more tubes end at the 66th floor.

187
00:14:46,680 --> 00:14:49,500
And three tubes drop off at the 90th floor,

188
00:14:49,960 --> 00:14:53,040
leaving only two remaining tubes to proceed to the top.

189
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:58,860
This bundled tube concept very easily enabled the architects

190
00:14:58,861 --> 00:15:02,900
to create this modulation of space as they went up.

191
00:15:03,440 --> 00:15:09,020
And the purpose of that was Sears required very large floors for their function,

192
00:15:09,260 --> 00:15:12,440
but they were only going to initially occupy half the building.

193
00:15:12,620 --> 00:15:17,300
The remaining half, or the upper half, was going to be leased to tenants.

194
00:15:17,301 --> 00:15:24,220
So the idea of dropping off these various tubes then resulted in a smaller floor plan,

195
00:15:24,340 --> 00:15:30,600
which gave more perimeter offices and was therefore much more attractive on the leasing market.

196
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:34,900
The bundled tubes, as well as the ingenious use of step-backs,

197
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:38,560
set the Sears Tower design apart from earlier skyscrapers.

198
00:15:39,780 --> 00:15:41,740
Breaking records was just an afterthought.

199
00:15:41,741 --> 00:15:48,160
The decision to become the tallest building was not part of the initial program.

200
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:54,960
But as you started to build floors that were smaller than the 50,000 square foot Sears floors,

201
00:15:55,040 --> 00:15:57,120
you started to build smaller ones to attract tenants,

202
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:01,180
you started to push upward to encompass the 3.5 million square feet.

203
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:06,040
And soon, the Skidmore, Owings and Merrill team found themselves with a structure

204
00:16:06,041 --> 00:16:08,420
that was just flirting with a world record.

205
00:16:08,421 --> 00:16:15,060
I went into a meeting with most of the Sears executives and the architects,

206
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:18,620
in which there were a number of cardboard models.

207
00:16:19,120 --> 00:16:22,340
And then out of a bag, Bruce, who was the consummate salesman,

208
00:16:22,480 --> 00:16:25,860
pulled out this walnut model and put it on the table

209
00:16:25,861 --> 00:16:29,200
and suggested that we were so close to the ultimate,

210
00:16:29,420 --> 00:16:31,340
so close to the world's dollars building,

211
00:16:31,540 --> 00:16:34,620
that we should consider going for the brass ring.

212
00:16:34,621 --> 00:16:40,720
and the Sears executives almost broke into applause.

213
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:42,760
I mean, there were smiles all around the table.

214
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:47,380
Just a few weeks later, on July 27, 1970,

215
00:16:47,980 --> 00:16:51,160
Sears management publicly unveiled the model for the Sears Tower.

216
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:56,840
A model for a building that would reach 1,450 feet into the sky.

217
00:16:57,660 --> 00:17:00,900
The model for the tallest building in the world.

218
00:17:01,440 --> 00:17:03,720
The public was pretty excited about it,

219
00:17:03,721 --> 00:17:09,800
I think most of the excitement came from the fact that we were one up on New York.

220
00:17:10,800 --> 00:17:12,960
Chicago was the birthplace of the skyscraper.

221
00:17:13,180 --> 00:17:15,520
It always prided itself on its tall buildings.

222
00:17:15,920 --> 00:17:17,780
And that tender was stolen by New York.

223
00:17:18,660 --> 00:17:22,220
The World Trade Center buildings were currently under construction in New York.

224
00:17:22,840 --> 00:17:25,060
They had been designed to be the tallest in the world.

225
00:17:26,060 --> 00:17:28,600
And now, the competition was on.

226
00:17:29,380 --> 00:17:32,140
And Chicago was poised to bring the title home.

227
00:17:33,720 --> 00:17:40,080
Ground was broken in August of 1970.

228
00:17:41,660 --> 00:17:43,880
We started almost immediately.

229
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:45,980
I didn't have finished working drawings.

230
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:50,200
I had finished working drawings close to the day

231
00:17:50,201 --> 00:17:53,400
that we would start that particular phase of the project

232
00:17:53,401 --> 00:17:56,660
so that the foundation drawings were finished before anything else,

233
00:17:56,800 --> 00:17:58,620
and that's when we started the foundations.

234
00:17:59,660 --> 00:18:03,180
By midwinter, work on the foundation site was well underway.

235
00:18:03,720 --> 00:18:12,720
The foundation plans called for a three-foot thick wall to be dug around the perimeter

236
00:18:12,721 --> 00:18:15,720
of the building site.

237
00:18:15,721 --> 00:18:21,720
Bentonite clay slurry, an aqueous mixture of insoluble clay, was poured into the excavation

238
00:18:21,721 --> 00:18:26,180
panels as they were dug to prevent ground collapse and water infiltration.

239
00:18:26,181 --> 00:18:30,040
The panels reached over five stories into the ground.

240
00:18:30,041 --> 00:18:34,480
The slurry was later displaced by concrete and pumped out of the foundation wall.

241
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:42,680
With the concrete foundation wall in place, the entire site was then excavated to the depth of over 50 feet.

242
00:18:44,060 --> 00:18:51,800
To complete the foundation, 114 reinforced concrete caissons, which varied in diameter from 6 to 10 feet,

243
00:18:52,360 --> 00:18:55,060
had to be dug an additional 50 feet into the earth.

244
00:18:55,061 --> 00:19:01,340
Only the tallest buildings in Chicago are required to go down to bedrock because of the load.

245
00:19:01,800 --> 00:19:07,780
And what these are, they're drilled shafts that extend down to the rock.

246
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:11,640
There's a steel jacket, and then they're filled with concrete.

247
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:15,060
And there's one of these located under each column.

248
00:19:16,140 --> 00:19:20,660
A five-foot-thick concrete mat was poured at the bottom of the excavation site.

249
00:19:21,780 --> 00:19:24,280
This mat tied all of the caissons together.

250
00:19:24,281 --> 00:19:27,060
and became the bottom floor of the Sears Tower.

251
00:19:28,140 --> 00:19:31,320
With the roots of the superstructure planted firmly in the ground,

252
00:19:32,140 --> 00:19:34,260
the Sears Tower was ready to soar.

253
00:19:36,960 --> 00:19:40,920
One of the architectural design ideas that was considered for the new Sears headquarters

254
00:19:40,921 --> 00:19:44,540
was a large square building that was just 80 stories tall.

255
00:19:45,100 --> 00:19:48,240
This short and stout design was referred to as the Pig.

256
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:52,360
The Sears Tower will return on Modern Marvels.

257
00:19:54,280 --> 00:20:01,660
By June 1971, with the Sears Tower Foundation firmly in place, the ironworkers were called

258
00:20:01,661 --> 00:20:09,040
in to do what they do best, build into the sky.

259
00:20:09,041 --> 00:20:13,240
Today these men still take pride in their contribution to the Chicago skyline.

260
00:20:13,241 --> 00:20:20,520
Mainly, the ironworkers on the Sears building are all members of Chicago local union number

261
00:20:20,521 --> 00:20:26,200
want, but there are also iron workers who came from all over the country who wanted

262
00:20:26,201 --> 00:20:30,860
the opportunity to work here and say, have the bragging rights to say they worked on

263
00:20:30,861 --> 00:20:33,020
the tallest building.

264
00:20:33,021 --> 00:20:39,900
Their jobs were made a little easier. In the interest of time and efficiency, the

265
00:20:39,901 --> 00:20:46,559
columns and girders would be welded together off-site at the fabrication plant.

266
00:20:46,560 --> 00:20:47,260
These

267
00:20:47,261 --> 00:20:53,160
These prefabricated tree units were delivered to the site to be raised and bolted together.

268
00:20:53,161 --> 00:20:55,160
The steel system was just wonderful.

269
00:20:55,161 --> 00:20:58,480
I mean, it went together as smooth as silk.

270
00:20:58,481 --> 00:21:06,600
The columns themselves had half of a girder attached to it so that you had the long column

271
00:21:06,601 --> 00:21:12,040
and then you had two floors of girders sticking out.

272
00:21:12,041 --> 00:21:14,420
Transporting the tree units was no small task.

273
00:21:14,421 --> 00:21:19,040
The largest width that could be transported through the streets of Chicago was 10 feet.

274
00:21:19,500 --> 00:21:22,520
This width was too narrow to be used effectively in the building.

275
00:21:24,260 --> 00:21:31,020
The fabricators came up with an ingenious plan of skewing the units at a 45-degree angle on specially modified trucks,

276
00:21:31,700 --> 00:21:34,900
with the girders hanging just inches off the ground between the tires.

277
00:21:37,720 --> 00:21:41,680
They could now increase the size of the units to 15 by 26 feet

278
00:21:41,681 --> 00:21:45,780
and still make the journey to the job site with little disruption to the city.

279
00:21:46,960 --> 00:21:51,160
These three units were brought directly from the fabrication shop.

280
00:21:51,600 --> 00:21:53,640
They pulled up at the base of the building,

281
00:21:54,160 --> 00:21:58,800
and these three units were directly lifted off the truck and erected immediately.

282
00:21:58,960 --> 00:22:00,480
There was no storage on the site.

283
00:22:00,960 --> 00:22:05,020
And the exercise was no more than putting together a whole series of tinker toys.

284
00:22:06,280 --> 00:22:10,020
Every piece was numbered and loaded on the truck at the right time

285
00:22:10,021 --> 00:22:13,240
so that it would be the next piece to go up on the building.

286
00:22:13,680 --> 00:22:20,560
So it had to take a lot of coordination between the fabricators and shipping and the truckers

287
00:22:20,561 --> 00:22:26,060
to make it all run smooth, and everything had to stay in line.

288
00:22:27,600 --> 00:22:31,360
Since the major work of welding the beams to the columns was done off-site,

289
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:36,080
the iron workers were able to easily accomplish an impressive two floors a week.

290
00:22:36,081 --> 00:22:43,140
the raising gang sets the steel they put one bolt in it the bolt up gang came up behind us

291
00:22:43,141 --> 00:22:47,360
stuck all the bolts in then the plumb up gang comes and they have an engineer with a transit

292
00:22:47,361 --> 00:22:50,220
and they straighten the building out because if they didn't it'd go out this way and that way

293
00:22:50,221 --> 00:22:54,600
and then once they got it all plumbed they tighten it up with the impact manager then

294
00:22:54,601 --> 00:22:58,780
the welders take over and that's how the building's put up everybody has to work as a team

295
00:22:58,781 --> 00:23:02,860
but the project would not be without its share of mishaps

296
00:23:03,840 --> 00:23:07,520
One of the incidents that took place during the construction of the building,

297
00:23:07,720 --> 00:23:12,660
we were erecting steel on about the 54th or 55th floor.

298
00:23:13,340 --> 00:23:16,840
And it was about 4.30 one afternoon, and my phone rang.

299
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:21,220
And our field representative was on the other end,

300
00:23:21,221 --> 00:23:23,740
and he said, I think you better come out to the building right away.

301
00:23:23,900 --> 00:23:24,900
Something happened here.

302
00:23:26,020 --> 00:23:29,440
The shiver, the pulley down in the basement exploded,

303
00:23:29,441 --> 00:23:36,480
and it cut the cable and it dropped a 7-ton column, 40 floors.

304
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:41,100
And the guys all heard that and they looked and they saw what happened

305
00:23:41,101 --> 00:23:43,640
and they all ran in and got under the building

306
00:23:43,641 --> 00:23:48,220
and the column came down under the pedestrian walkway

307
00:23:48,221 --> 00:23:53,020
and some of the cable hit the taxi cab out in the street.

308
00:23:54,460 --> 00:23:57,320
The superintendent got a bunch of iron workers

309
00:23:57,321 --> 00:24:04,840
They got that cab out of there and they got everything straightened out so that it wouldn't make a big scene for the news media

310
00:24:04,841 --> 00:24:09,940
It was right at 430. Everybody was leaving work, you know all the office people

311
00:24:09,941 --> 00:24:11,940
That poor cab driver

312
00:24:11,941 --> 00:24:13,691
He had his hands on the wheel.

313
00:24:13,692 --> 00:24:19,120
You know only can we can get his fingers off the wheel because all the cable attacked his cab

314
00:24:19,121 --> 00:24:21,760
He just happened to be just sitting there in traffic and all of a sudden

315
00:24:21,761 --> 00:24:24,880
and boom, he's getting eaten up by this cable.

316
00:24:25,440 --> 00:24:28,940
They said the woman, the passenger that was in the back seat, wet her pants.

317
00:24:32,080 --> 00:24:36,720
The amazing part of this story is no one was hurt, no one was scratched.

318
00:24:36,721 --> 00:24:40,860
The cable landed on the hood of a taxi cab, and that was the worst thing that happened.

319
00:24:42,020 --> 00:24:45,720
Other accidents later in the construction had more tragic results.

320
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:51,620
There were a total of six deaths on the entire project, including the death of one iron worker.

321
00:24:51,760 --> 00:24:57,760
By the summer of 1972, with over half the building below them and hundreds of men working

322
00:24:57,761 --> 00:25:03,000
on the project, it would take sharp planning and exemplary resourcefulness to keep the

323
00:25:03,001 --> 00:25:05,400
project on the fast track.

324
00:25:05,401 --> 00:25:11,120
We needed the cooperation of the unions who would work on a staggered start, staggered

325
00:25:11,121 --> 00:25:13,360
stop, staggered lunch.

326
00:25:13,361 --> 00:25:18,560
You needed the cooperation of the men who were willing to take meals off of carts that

327
00:25:18,561 --> 00:25:21,240
that we brought up into the building.

328
00:25:21,241 --> 00:25:23,800
Medical staff, we had our own medical staff

329
00:25:23,801 --> 00:25:26,500
and first aid offices throughout the building.

330
00:25:26,501 --> 00:25:29,140
And other methodology that was utilized

331
00:25:29,141 --> 00:25:32,500
kept the building on target from a timing standpoint.

332
00:25:32,501 --> 00:25:35,740
Otherwise, if it had taken you another day afloor,

333
00:25:35,741 --> 00:25:37,560
you could see what the difference would have been

334
00:25:37,561 --> 00:25:39,740
with regard to the overall schedule.

335
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:43,020
The faster the iron workers set the skeleton

336
00:25:43,021 --> 00:25:45,660
of the building, the sooner the other trades could get in.

337
00:25:45,661 --> 00:25:50,680
I believe you broke a lot of records for tonnage set in one day.

338
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:57,960
I remember one day when we actually set over 100 truckloads in one day.

339
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:02,180
Yeah, from the top of the building, if you looked south onto Canal Street,

340
00:26:02,520 --> 00:26:05,900
the trucks were lined up because you couldn't block them downtown streets.

341
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:09,380
It looked like Rommel's Army in World War II with a line of trucks.

342
00:26:10,260 --> 00:26:13,240
And they'd come in, then we'd unload it, boom, that truck would go,

343
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:14,440
they'd call in another truck.

344
00:26:15,660 --> 00:26:18,720
It was something. It's something I'll always remember.

345
00:26:19,480 --> 00:26:24,020
As the building climbed, the workers found themselves fighting the forces of Mother Nature

346
00:26:24,021 --> 00:26:26,540
in their struggle to claim their share of the sky.

347
00:26:28,540 --> 00:26:32,960
They found that the weather at the top of the building could be drastically different from that on the ground.

348
00:26:33,900 --> 00:26:40,700
In the morning, the ironworker steward would go up early and check the weather on top

349
00:26:40,701 --> 00:26:42,840
to see if it was fit to work.

350
00:26:42,841 --> 00:26:49,260
and sometimes it would be nice down below and sun shining and not a lot of wind

351
00:26:49,261 --> 00:26:52,700
and the steward would come down and say, no work today, boys.

352
00:26:52,780 --> 00:26:59,760
The wind is blowing too strong up there or it's maybe 20 degrees colder

353
00:26:59,761 --> 00:27:02,660
and it was actually too cold to work.

354
00:27:04,040 --> 00:27:09,100
Dozens of work days were lost due to wind, snow, or temperatures below 10 degrees.

355
00:27:09,101 --> 00:27:16,060
From August to December 1972, iron workers were unable to work a complete week.

356
00:27:17,880 --> 00:27:23,560
Yet even with the setbacks, the structure continued on its journey to top the world.

357
00:27:24,240 --> 00:27:28,880
During the whole process of building this, we would get reports from New York

358
00:27:28,881 --> 00:27:32,660
that were saying that they're going to add floors or a penthouse

359
00:27:32,661 --> 00:27:35,040
to one of the World Trade Center buildings.

360
00:27:35,041 --> 00:27:39,860
and that building was going to surpass Sears in its height,

361
00:27:39,980 --> 00:27:41,180
but that never happened.

362
00:27:41,360 --> 00:27:42,320
It was just a rumor.

363
00:27:43,520 --> 00:27:46,840
And fittingly, on an abnormally cold and windy day,

364
00:27:47,360 --> 00:27:49,120
May 3rd, 1973,

365
00:27:49,920 --> 00:27:52,620
the building was ceremoniously topped off.

366
00:27:54,060 --> 00:27:58,080
The topping out ceremony involved the man, Mayor Daley,

367
00:27:58,240 --> 00:27:59,800
who was like God in Chicago,

368
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:01,880
and all of the top Sears executives.

369
00:28:02,160 --> 00:28:04,520
We had some Congress people, some show business people.

370
00:28:04,521 --> 00:28:09,000
It was a very big ceremony because this was the largest building in the world,

371
00:28:09,200 --> 00:28:13,640
and the mayor was very proud of it, Sears was very proud of it, and it was a big deal.

372
00:28:14,320 --> 00:28:17,360
But center stage in the ceremony was the final beam.

373
00:28:18,600 --> 00:28:23,400
Painted white and signed by over 15,000 Sears employees and construction workers,

374
00:28:24,380 --> 00:28:26,660
the final beam was hoisted up the building.

375
00:28:29,380 --> 00:28:31,860
I happened to be running the engine that day,

376
00:28:31,861 --> 00:28:36,340
And when I got to 76th floor, I figured, well, I'm in control.

377
00:28:36,540 --> 00:28:42,020
So I put the brake on, I stopped it, and I went out there and I took a picture of the beam,

378
00:28:42,200 --> 00:28:46,200
got back in the cab and raised it up again and took it to the top.

379
00:28:48,380 --> 00:28:50,620
The job of the iron workers was completed.

380
00:28:51,560 --> 00:28:57,040
And the skeleton of the Sears Tower stood triumphantly, 1450 feet into the sky.

381
00:28:57,041 --> 00:29:05,500
I believe it was said that there was well over a thousand construction workers on the job at one time,

382
00:29:05,620 --> 00:29:07,160
all working at the different trades.

383
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:11,260
That's how large of a scope of this project was.

384
00:29:12,100 --> 00:29:16,720
The tower was now standing, but it was far from complete.

385
00:29:19,720 --> 00:29:22,020
The Sears Tower is not perfectly vertical.

386
00:29:22,880 --> 00:29:24,800
The building leans six inches to the west.

387
00:29:24,801 --> 00:29:28,960
this anticipated lean was caused by the unbalanced distribution of weight

388
00:29:28,961 --> 00:29:30,980
resulting from the asymmetrical design.

389
00:29:32,200 --> 00:29:35,240
The Sears Tower will return on Modern Marvels.

390
00:29:38,240 --> 00:29:41,260
As the skeleton of the Sears Tower climbed skyward,

391
00:29:42,180 --> 00:29:45,220
legions of tradesmen waited in the wings for their turn with the building.

392
00:29:46,040 --> 00:29:50,140
There's a positive side to the higher building and the larger project.

393
00:29:50,141 --> 00:29:57,200
The top people at all of the companies working on the project were assigned to this particular project.

394
00:29:57,960 --> 00:30:01,020
I was dealing with the chairman of Alcoa.

395
00:30:01,240 --> 00:30:04,480
I was dealing with the president and chairman of American Bridge.

396
00:30:05,520 --> 00:30:12,420
Every contractor was the top executive or chief operating officer of the organization.

397
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:16,040
When we wanted to get something done, we could get it done.

398
00:30:16,041 --> 00:30:22,040
Following on the heels of the ironworkers, work began on the floors.

399
00:30:22,041 --> 00:30:30,040
In a tubular or a bundled tube structure, it's important to distribute the loads,

400
00:30:30,041 --> 00:30:34,040
the gravity loads from the building, the dead load of the floor system and the people,

401
00:30:34,041 --> 00:30:40,040
the live load of the people, to distribute it as equally as you can throughout the structure.

402
00:30:40,041 --> 00:30:45,040
So the idea was every five floors rotate the framing 90 degrees.

403
00:30:45,041 --> 00:30:51,360
So it would be in the north-south direction for five floors, and then it would switch to the east-west direction for another five floors.

404
00:30:52,620 --> 00:30:59,920
The lower floors enclosed just over 50,000 square feet of space, a little more than the size of a football field.

405
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:10,380
The floor's sizes were diminished at the 50th floor, 66th floor, and at the 90th floor,

406
00:31:10,800 --> 00:31:14,840
leaving the top floors over 75% smaller than the ground floors.

407
00:31:15,040 --> 00:31:17,760
at only 12,283 square feet.

408
00:31:20,580 --> 00:31:24,800
Five sets of two-story tall floors were strategically placed throughout the building.

409
00:31:25,740 --> 00:31:27,520
They were included for two reasons,

410
00:31:27,940 --> 00:31:29,940
to house the immense mechanical systems

411
00:31:29,941 --> 00:31:32,780
and to provide additional integrity to the structure.

412
00:31:33,740 --> 00:31:38,240
Structurally, we found that we could take a lot of advantage

413
00:31:38,241 --> 00:31:43,740
of those double-height floors by X-bracing between the columns,

414
00:31:43,741 --> 00:31:48,120
and it acted like a stiffening element as you went up the building.

415
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:53,240
So by using a very small amount of structural steel for these diagonals,

416
00:31:53,380 --> 00:32:00,000
we were able to reduce the lateral drift due to wind by about 15%, which is very significant.

417
00:32:02,340 --> 00:32:05,780
Next, the steel superstructure was coated with fire retardant.

418
00:32:06,040 --> 00:32:08,860
We didn't use asbestos-based fireproofing.

419
00:32:08,940 --> 00:32:10,640
Even though we could have, and it was cheaper,

420
00:32:10,641 --> 00:32:16,080
a decision was made that asbestos could potentially cause cancer.

421
00:32:16,380 --> 00:32:18,340
Let's not use asbestos in this building.

422
00:32:18,460 --> 00:32:21,480
So we didn't, and that was a very prudent decision as it turned out.

423
00:32:22,860 --> 00:32:26,020
It was now time to put a skin on the structural steel.

424
00:32:27,260 --> 00:32:31,380
Architect Bruce Graham had envisioned a sleek black building made of anodized aluminum.

425
00:32:32,180 --> 00:32:36,980
The price for the aluminum curtain wall was way over budget.

426
00:32:36,981 --> 00:32:40,900
I mean, big percentage marks over the budget.

427
00:32:42,140 --> 00:32:44,200
Graham reluctantly looked into other options.

428
00:32:44,980 --> 00:32:49,140
An Italian stone supplier approached the project team about using granite for the outer wall,

429
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:52,440
which the company could offer at an economical price.

430
00:32:54,040 --> 00:32:58,880
Bruce and I went over to Rome, and we verified on that basis.

431
00:33:00,160 --> 00:33:03,440
In the meantime, I was hearing from my New York office

432
00:33:03,441 --> 00:33:07,760
that there were calls coming through from the aluminum suppliers

433
00:33:07,761 --> 00:33:11,040
asking what Richard and Bruce were doing in Italy.

434
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:15,720
And mystically, when we returned to the United States,

435
00:33:16,300 --> 00:33:19,320
there were new aluminum prices that showed up

436
00:33:19,321 --> 00:33:23,780
that were quite a bit less than the granite wall,

437
00:33:23,781 --> 00:33:28,280
and that's why the building is aluminum today and not granite.

438
00:33:29,720 --> 00:33:31,500
With the aluminum secured,

439
00:33:31,501 --> 00:33:39,000
bronze tinted windows were chosen to accent the sleek black facade over 16

440
00:33:39,001 --> 00:33:43,620
thousand windows were installed to complete the exterior

441
00:33:43,621 --> 00:33:49,200
as crews worked feverishly to stay on schedule engineers had to come up with

442
00:33:49,201 --> 00:33:52,860
an efficient way to transport the thousands of future tenants and visitors

443
00:33:52,861 --> 00:33:59,940
to the top of the world the building contains 104 elevators including 14

444
00:33:59,941 --> 00:34:04,380
double-deckers. These double-decker elevators carry passengers to the sky

445
00:34:04,381 --> 00:34:11,100
lobbies on the 33rd and 34th floors and the 66th and 67th floors where they can

446
00:34:11,101 --> 00:34:17,040
transfer to local elevators. Due to its sheer size, the tower had some other

447
00:34:17,041 --> 00:34:21,960
extraordinary problems that needed to be addressed. The unique phenomenon in

448
00:34:21,961 --> 00:34:27,360
super tall buildings is stack effect. Essentially, hot air rises and is

449
00:34:27,361 --> 00:34:30,315
replaced by cooler air from the outside of the building.

450
00:34:30,316 --> 00:34:32,320
The profound difference in temperature

451
00:34:32,321 --> 00:34:37,560
causes air on the ground floor to be sucked into the building, creating a massive draft.

452
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:43,207
The interior air in the building in the wintertime is heated.

453
00:34:43,208 --> 00:34:45,620
The exterior air is very cold,

454
00:34:46,180 --> 00:34:49,879
and therefore the warmer air in the building begins to rise.

455
00:34:49,880 --> 00:34:51,420
Besides being unpleasant,

456
00:34:52,240 --> 00:34:57,020
the draft affects many of the operational systems in the building, such as the elevators and the

457
00:34:57,021 --> 00:34:58,234
entrance doors.

458
00:34:58,235 --> 00:35:04,060
To minimize the problem, the lobby was designed with revolving doors. An

459
00:35:04,061 --> 00:35:08,380
integrated airlock system was incorporated at the loading docks to control the amount

460
00:35:08,381 --> 00:35:11,880
of air coming in at the ground floor.

461
00:35:11,881 --> 00:35:16,660
As you bring in material from the loading dock, one set of doors open, you move the

462
00:35:16,661 --> 00:35:23,120
material into the airlock, that set of doors closes, then the second set of doors open

463
00:35:23,121 --> 00:35:26,300
on the interior of the building and you can move the goods into the building.

464
00:35:26,440 --> 00:35:32,620
So there's never a continuous path of open air leading into the building.

465
00:35:32,800 --> 00:35:33,780
It's always sealed.

466
00:35:34,660 --> 00:35:38,920
With the smallest of details secured, in the fall of 1974,

467
00:35:39,500 --> 00:35:43,680
just three and a half years after construction began, the Sears Tower was complete.

468
00:35:45,020 --> 00:35:48,540
The key words are in my business on time and on budget,

469
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:52,360
and we were under on each by a very small margin.

470
00:35:52,361 --> 00:35:57,880
But the building was right on. It was incredible that it could be done.

471
00:35:58,000 --> 00:36:04,240
This was without computers, and the schedule worked very well.

472
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:08,540
Sears Roebuck and company could finally inhabit their new home.

473
00:36:09,980 --> 00:36:14,420
However, their days in their new state-of-the-art headquarters were numbered.

474
00:36:14,421 --> 00:36:20,940
The Sears Tower contains 2 million cubic feet of concrete,

475
00:36:21,380 --> 00:36:24,320
enough to build an 8-lane freeway 5 miles long.

476
00:36:25,260 --> 00:36:28,080
The Sears Tower will return on Modern Marvels.

477
00:36:33,300 --> 00:36:38,240
By 1974, the world's largest store, Sears Roebuck & Company,

478
00:36:38,700 --> 00:36:41,180
was now headquartered in the world's tallest building.

479
00:36:41,181 --> 00:36:47,120
approximately 40% of the building was occupied by about 8,000 Sears workers.

480
00:36:48,760 --> 00:36:54,880
Ahead of its time, it was one of the first office spaces with robotic mail carriers buzzing through the halls

481
00:36:54,881 --> 00:37:01,560
and a contemporary interior that was scientifically designed to be the most efficient workplace ever.

482
00:37:02,280 --> 00:37:10,740
We thought we did a marvelous job of locating departments that needed to be near each other

483
00:37:10,741 --> 00:37:12,480
next to each other.

484
00:37:12,780 --> 00:37:15,380
With huge 50,000 square foot floors,

485
00:37:15,960 --> 00:37:18,240
departments were defined with specific color schemes

486
00:37:18,241 --> 00:37:20,040
and interchangeable furniture.

487
00:37:21,100 --> 00:37:24,120
But despite the best intentions of the interior designers

488
00:37:24,121 --> 00:37:25,200
and the planning committee,

489
00:37:25,780 --> 00:37:27,100
there were problems.

490
00:37:28,040 --> 00:37:30,280
Some floors were so large that some employees

491
00:37:30,281 --> 00:37:32,500
never had an opportunity to look out a window.

492
00:37:34,300 --> 00:37:37,520
And moving from the horizontal sprawl of the Great Works

493
00:37:37,521 --> 00:37:40,360
to the vertical skyscraper of the Sears Tower,

494
00:37:40,740 --> 00:37:42,940
was a major adjustment for Sears employees.

495
00:37:44,520 --> 00:37:47,020
Some 30- and 40-year-long friendships ended

496
00:37:47,021 --> 00:37:49,140
because of the upright configuration of the tower.

497
00:37:50,260 --> 00:37:53,280
Some employees only saw people who rode in their elevators.

498
00:37:54,140 --> 00:37:56,600
I'd talked to people who worked in the Sears Tower,

499
00:37:56,700 --> 00:37:59,140
and that's one of the things that they had mentioned to me consistently

500
00:37:59,141 --> 00:38:03,100
was that, you know, they sort of lost track of other people in the company

501
00:38:03,101 --> 00:38:04,960
being in the tower because sometimes you had to hop over

502
00:38:04,961 --> 00:38:07,460
two different elevator banks to get to another floor.

503
00:38:07,900 --> 00:38:09,520
Employees lost touch with each other.

504
00:38:10,340 --> 00:38:14,580
And more ominously, the company started losing touch with its customers.

505
00:38:15,720 --> 00:38:18,520
Other retailers were nipping at our heels all the time.

506
00:38:19,060 --> 00:38:22,340
And Kmart got bigger than Sears and Walmart got bigger than Sears.

507
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:26,240
So by the time we were moving in, we were still number one.

508
00:38:26,380 --> 00:38:27,940
But boy, these guys were right behind us.

509
00:38:28,640 --> 00:38:34,380
Based on projected growth figures, by 1998, Sears would occupy all 110 floors.

510
00:38:35,780 --> 00:38:38,720
Until then, tenants would fill the upper floors.

511
00:38:39,320 --> 00:38:42,060
The growth that we had seen did not occur.

512
00:38:42,180 --> 00:38:42,900
It never occurred.

513
00:38:43,040 --> 00:38:45,360
We never got much beyond 2 million square feet.

514
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:47,540
What they do is they lease the extra floors out,

515
00:38:47,840 --> 00:38:50,460
and so it just brought a lot of new businesses and firms

516
00:38:50,461 --> 00:38:53,420
and economic activity to the Chicago area,

517
00:38:53,520 --> 00:38:55,760
so the Sears Tower really had a dramatic impact.

518
00:38:55,761 --> 00:38:59,400
The one seedy area surrounding the Sears Tower was now booming.

519
00:39:00,520 --> 00:39:03,020
But Sears, the retailer, was not.

520
00:39:03,021 --> 00:39:10,660
In 1988, the Sears Tower that was built for $175 million in 1974

521
00:39:10,661 --> 00:39:14,240
was put on the market for $1 billion.

522
00:39:15,160 --> 00:39:18,940
People thought that Sears was too big for anybody ever to try to buy,

523
00:39:19,160 --> 00:39:20,440
to strip them of their assets.

524
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:24,180
But then came the R.J. Reynolds and a couple of other major buys.

525
00:39:24,740 --> 00:39:28,460
People put together deals with junk bonds and a lot of other financing

526
00:39:28,461 --> 00:39:32,280
so that it now became possible that somebody would buy Sears

527
00:39:32,281 --> 00:39:35,820
and strip off the tower and some other assets.

528
00:39:36,520 --> 00:39:38,140
Therefore, the Sears board decided,

529
00:39:38,480 --> 00:39:42,600
let's sell the tower and take the money and buy some of our stock back.

530
00:39:42,720 --> 00:39:44,320
Let's put it into improving the stores.

531
00:39:45,420 --> 00:39:48,480
After only 16 years in their custom-made headquarters,

532
00:39:49,820 --> 00:39:53,880
Sears moved out of the tower and into a complex in the Chicago suburbs.

533
00:39:53,881 --> 00:40:00,120
With Sears leaving almost the first 50 floors empty

534
00:40:00,121 --> 00:40:04,460
The new owners made a series of renovations to attract new tenants

535
00:40:04,461 --> 00:40:09,440
In 1990 when Sears sold the building and moved out

536
00:40:09,441 --> 00:40:12,580
The building was no longer a corporate headquarters building

537
00:40:12,581 --> 00:40:14,220
It was a leased building

538
00:40:14,221 --> 00:40:17,920
It would be 100% leased to tenants

539
00:40:17,921 --> 00:40:23,860
And it became important now that you could directly access the elevators

540
00:40:23,861 --> 00:40:25,160
from the lobby.

541
00:40:26,320 --> 00:40:28,440
The elevator system was revised,

542
00:40:28,860 --> 00:40:30,640
and the entire lobby was redesigned.

543
00:40:31,660 --> 00:40:35,200
Some of the utilitarian travertine and plastic laminate

544
00:40:35,201 --> 00:40:37,660
that Sears had installed initially was replaced

545
00:40:37,661 --> 00:40:39,540
with stainless steel and granite.

546
00:40:41,140 --> 00:40:46,320
And also a much more obvious entry to the sky deck was provided

547
00:40:46,321 --> 00:40:48,180
to try and encourage that business,

548
00:40:48,181 --> 00:40:52,140
which is a very profitable part of the operation of the building.

549
00:40:52,141 --> 00:40:55,740
But the lobbies went through yet another transformation.

550
00:40:56,540 --> 00:41:02,560
After terrorists attacked the World Trade Center towers in New York City on September 11, 2001.

551
00:41:03,560 --> 00:41:07,640
Our security after 9-1-1 has changed dramatically.

552
00:41:08,400 --> 00:41:15,700
We have responded with upgrading our security because our tenants needed to have a sense of comfort.

553
00:41:15,701 --> 00:41:24,300
Well, the fancy security features that you have here are a lot of x-ray machines, metal detectors, and that's really for the safety of the people.

554
00:41:24,780 --> 00:41:33,735
It doesn't diminish from what we do as for the friendly, proactive attitude we have, but it does give people a sense of when they walk into the Sears Tower, we are

555
00:41:33,736 --> 00:41:34,436
secure.

556
00:41:35,880 --> 00:41:43,180
Employees now gain access to their elevator banks with a sophisticated card access system, which opens sliding glass gates.

557
00:41:43,181 --> 00:41:47,980
while improvements to the lobbies were made through the years

558
00:41:47,981 --> 00:41:50,580
the roof had its own additions

559
00:41:50,581 --> 00:41:54,340
Sears Tower was upgraded in 2000

560
00:41:54,341 --> 00:41:58,060
with four high-definition digital television combination antennas

561
00:41:58,061 --> 00:42:03,340
an air crane was used to lift the load

562
00:42:03,341 --> 00:42:05,360
from the top of the parking garage skyward

563
00:42:05,361 --> 00:42:06,860
about one quarter mile

564
00:42:06,861 --> 00:42:14,260
Being the highest point in the Chicago metropolitan area

565
00:42:14,261 --> 00:42:18,080
and allowing reception that reaches a 60 to 80 mile radius,

566
00:42:18,600 --> 00:42:21,620
the top of the Sears Tower has always been prime real estate.

567
00:42:22,940 --> 00:42:25,440
The Sears building is very unique in its design.

568
00:42:25,780 --> 00:42:29,360
Because it's so big, it's called vertical real estate.

569
00:42:30,060 --> 00:42:31,720
And when you're in an area like this,

570
00:42:32,000 --> 00:42:36,180
anybody that has a broadcast system or a two-way system

571
00:42:36,181 --> 00:42:39,640
or any kind of an RF where they want a wireless system put in,

572
00:42:40,340 --> 00:42:43,500
the Sears building was built with that in mind.

573
00:42:43,900 --> 00:42:45,680
So they capitalize on it.

574
00:42:47,540 --> 00:42:51,360
Years ago, when the Sears Tower and Petronas Towers in Malaysia

575
00:42:51,361 --> 00:42:53,960
competed for the tallest building in the world title,

576
00:42:54,840 --> 00:42:58,720
the Sears Tower antennas arguably put the Sears Tower on top.

577
00:42:59,740 --> 00:43:04,460
However, in 2004, a skyscraper in Taiwan was erected

578
00:43:04,461 --> 00:43:06,920
and captured all the record titles.

579
00:43:08,100 --> 00:43:09,640
Well, almost all.

580
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:15,100
The Type A 101 building is certainly considered now the tallest building in the world.

581
00:43:15,680 --> 00:43:19,280
It is taller than the Sears Tower, you know, roof line.

582
00:43:19,440 --> 00:43:22,380
I think by about 30 feet, if I'm not mistaken.

583
00:43:22,880 --> 00:43:25,080
So we will give them credit for that.

584
00:43:25,180 --> 00:43:29,540
As far as our antennas, though, we still have the tallest antennas in the world.

585
00:43:30,060 --> 00:43:33,520
And those extend 287 feet above the roof line.

586
00:43:34,460 --> 00:43:41,000
But with or without the antennas, the Sears Tower retains the name of the company that dreamt big enough to build it

587
00:43:41,001 --> 00:43:45,520
and remains a triumph for the people who worked on it and worked in it.

588
00:43:46,460 --> 00:43:53,120
It's great when I can look out from 10 miles away and see a Sears Tower standing so tall,

589
00:43:53,260 --> 00:43:57,020
and it's great to feel that you were a part of it.

590
00:43:58,760 --> 00:44:03,700
What makes Sears Tower what it is is the actual character of the building itself.

591
00:44:04,460 --> 00:44:08,680
I'm here. Look at me. I stand above everything else that's not there.

592
00:44:09,340 --> 00:44:11,240
It's become a symbol of Chicago.

593
00:44:11,460 --> 00:44:15,140
Almost any time you see a picture of Chicago, you might see the Hancock Building,

594
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:17,260
but for sure you're going to see the Sears Tower.

595
00:44:18,100 --> 00:44:22,520
Still, it's the tallest building in North America, and we can't lose sight of that.

